/// The 'constructor' keyword decides, and the name does not.
///
/// Exactly inverted today.  'function Init' IS a constructor -- calling the
/// class name runs it -- and 'constructor Make' is not, so Made(7) fails with
/// 'Expected 0 arguments but got 1.'  Every constructor semantic keys on the
/// lexeme 'Init': ObjClass.Call looks up FindOverload('Init', ...), Resolver
/// checks Name.Lexeme = 'Init', Interpreter sets IsInitializer from the same
/// comparison.  The keyword is parsed and then dropped.
///
/// ⚠️ This is not a new design.  Parser.a24 already carries the rule as a
/// comment -- 'A constructor is spelled ''constructor'', not named ''init''.'
/// -- next to the code that consumes the keyword and discards it.  The intent
/// was recorded and never wired up.
///
/// ⚠️ Turbo Pascal is the model and is the same way round: there the KEYWORD
/// makes a constructor and the name is convention, which is why New(P, Init)
/// names it at the call.  Algol-24 inverted both halves.
///
/// Migration costs nothing: all 63 constructors in the corpus are already
/// spelled 'constructor Init', and no method anywhere is named Init without it.
///
/// Fails with: Can't return a value from an initializer.  (file rejected -- no
/// test ran) -- a sharper demonstration than expected.  The ordinary method
/// 'function Init (N : Integer) : Integer' is refused a result type, because
/// today the name alone makes it an initializer.
///
class Made;
var
    N : Integer := 0;

begin
    constructor Make (N : Integer);
    begin
        this.N := N;
    end

    function Init (N : Integer) : Integer;
    begin
        Exit N * 2;
    end
end

test 'The constructor keyword decides, not the name';
begin
    var Thing := Made (7);

    AssertEqual (7,  Thing.N);
    AssertEqual (14, Thing.Init (7));
end
